+# Spine authoring in a lane
+
+The two lane modes of the `spine-authoring` skill ([../SKILL.md](../SKILL.md),
+"Three ways a spine grows"). Read this before authoring or judging a lane's
+spine change. The tier questions in the skill's §1–§3 still apply to every
+row you write.
+
+## In a lane, released rungs: one change set, authored and judged whole
+
+An open set converges one missing link per sitting: a child is returned for
+want of a parent, then a TC for an arm its LLR states. A closed set is judged
+once.
+
+- **Name the anchor first.** This is the approved need or requirement the
+  change hangs from. If no approved row demands the change, add a derived SR
+  (the skill's §2(c)) under the anchor, labelled with its lens from the start rather than
+  when a child is returned for want of a parent.
+- **Author the subtree in one pass.** Include every new and amended row from
+  the anchor down (SRs, LLRs, TCs and interface rows) and each approved row
+  whose text the change makes untrue. New rows stay `Drafted`; interface rows
+  carry no approval status. Put the skill's tier questions (§1–§3) to its rows as you
+  write them.
+- **Map the arms.** For each LLR, list what it states (each outcome, refusal
+  and exemption) and the TC clause that verifies each. A TC covering only
+  some of its LLR's arms is the commonest return, and no lexical check sees
+  it.
+- **Write the set against settled code.** A row written while the code still
+  moves is re-judged each time the code does. While the code iterates, keep
+  only the trace fields current on each commit (`Implements:`, the LLR's
+  module and symbols, the TC `Evidence`) and list the row changes the code
+  implies. Write those changes once review has stopped moving the code. This
+  is the lane-level case of `docs/process.md` §4's "sequence requirement-text
+  work *into* an open window". A Done-when edit is the exception: it holds the
+  lane's next build until its exact text is blessed, so judge it when it is
+  made.
+- **Close the edges before the sitting.**
+  - Each row's parent is in the set or already approved.
+  - Each TC names its LLR and that LLR's SR.
+  - Each interface row the change adds or alters has a citing TC.
+  - `scripts/trace.py --strict-integrity` and
+    `scripts/check_trajectory.py --strict` pass. They check the whole repo,
+    and neither proves arm coverage; the arms map does.
+- **Judge it in one sitting, top-down.** One combined adjudication (the
+  `combined` brief) takes the set's amendments, first approvals and any
+  Done-when change together. Read parents before children. A returned parent
+  carries its children with it: name in the verdict which children are
+  returned only for their chain, so they are rewritten with the parent, not
+  on their own.
+
+## In a lane, a held rung: split the set at the dial
+
+- **Author the held rows like the rest of the set,** but leave them `Drafted`.
+  They reach the owner through the approval brief, never through the lane's
+  sitting.
+- **Re-attest only what is clarity.** On a held rung the sitting may carry an
+  approved row's amendment over only when it rules the change CLARITY, and
+  says so in the verdict. A MEANING change stays the owner's.
+- **Released children of a held row wait.** They are judged with the lane but
+  approved only once their held parent is, by the rule above.
